    "created_at": "2026-11-06T14:44:28Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-11-06T14:44:28Z",
    "body": "> I'm not overly wedded to one way of doing things so [@domenic](https://github.com/domenic) if your DOM PR for example patches more than my one I'm happy to close mine and go with yours.\n\nI would love for you (or someone else who is getting paid) to take over this work :). Please feel free to use or disregard my branches as you see fit.\n\n> Yours does seem to do more iterating but I understand that's because of \"an ancestor’s hooks can change a descendant’s form owner. Keep custom element upgrade handling at its existing point in insertion.\".?\n> \n> From what I can tell though browsers don't actually do the subtree first behaviour they do the per node behaviour.\n\nBasically it seems like there's a tradeoff between efficiency/complexity and consistent callback ordering. For example (**case 4**):\n\n```html\n<div id=\"wrapper\">\n  <form id=\"f\"></form>\n  <x-control form=\"f\"></x-control>\n</div>\n<script>\nwrapper.remove();\n</script>\n```\n\nThe single-iteration version will give associated(null) → disconnected, whereas the two-iteration version will give disconnected → associated(null). That is, the single-iteration version breaks the \"most fundamental first\" principle I proposed.\n\nBut yeah, apparently all browsers agree with associated(null) → disconnected in this case. And single-iteration is more efficient. So probably we should go with single-iteration.\n\nThis makes things more fragile for authors: for example authors might write their `disconnectedCallback` in a way that assumes form associations are not yet broken, based on their experience with case 1, and then their code gets broken by case 4. That's unfortunate. But we can probably deal with it.",
